CAT = "sgsnbTeam"; SEG = "TEA"
Q = [
('Who remains accountable for a task a registered nurse delegates?',
 'The registered nurse who delegated', ['Only the unlicensed assistant who did it', "The patient's family members", 'The pharmacy department'],
 'Delegation transfers the task but not accountability; the nurse remains accountable for judging whether the task is appropriate and for supervising it.'),
('Which task may a registered nurse safely delegate to a trained healthcare assistant?',
 'Taking routine vital signs when stable', ["Assessing a new patient's pain", 'Interpreting an abnormal ECG tracing', "Planning the patient's discharge teaching"],
 "Routine measurements in stable patients are delegable; assessment, interpretation and care planning need a nurse's judgement."),
('Before delegating a task, which factor should the nurse consider first?',
 "The patient's needs and the assistant's skill", ['Which person is the least busy', 'Which task is the most disliked', 'The seniority of the assistant on the ward roster'],
 "Delegation depends on the patient's needs and risk, the task's complexity and the competence of the person receiving it."),
('A nurse assigns tasks. Which patient should the most experienced nurse be assigned?',
 'A newly unstable postoperative patient', ['A patient awaiting discharge later today', 'A patient with a minor dressing', 'A patient who needs a bed bath'],
 'Unstable or complex patients are given to staff with the competence to assess and respond; stable routine care is distributed accordingly.'),
('What is the best response when a colleague says she disagrees with the care plan?',
 'Discuss it openly and respectfully', ['Ignore her comment altogether', 'Talk about her to other staff', 'Change the plan without telling her'],
 "Respectful collaboration with the team, including raising and resolving differences, keeps the patient's interests first."),
('What is the purpose of a multidisciplinary team meeting?',
 'To coordinate care with all disciplines', ["To agree next month's duty roster", 'To approve changes to ward staffing levels', 'To review staff leave requests'],
 "Teams from different disciplines share information and agree on goals and responsibilities for a patient's care."),
('A newly graduated nurse is unsure about a new pump. What is the most appropriate action?',
 'Ask for training or supervision first', ['Use it with the leaflet only', 'Ask a student to set it up', 'Use it only on stable, low-risk patients first'],
 'Nurses must achieve competence before taking on a new role or equipment, and ask for support when unsure.'),
('Which statement best describes continuing professional education (CPE)?',
 'Ongoing learning to keep practice current', ['A one-time exam taken after graduation', 'A task for managers only', 'An optional activity for senior nurses only'],
 'The Code expects nurses to maintain competence through continuing education, quality improvement and lifelong learning.'),
('Which activity is most likely to count as CPE?',
 'Attending an accredited clinical workshop', ['Reading a general newspaper daily', 'Attending a staff social event', 'Attending a routine ward roster briefing meeting'],
 'Structured learning relevant to practice, such as accredited courses, workshops and supervised learning, supports currency of practice.'),
('A nurse returning after five years away wants to restart clinical work. What is the most appropriate action?',
 'Complete required return-to-practice training', ['Start work without any review', 'Rely on her older certificates', 'Shadow staff for a single shift, then start work'],
 'Currency of practice requires updating knowledge and skills before returning to clinical work, in line with regulatory requirements.'),
('Which method gives the most useful feedback to a junior colleague?',
 'Specific, honest, constructive comments', ['General praise only', 'Criticism in front of others', 'No feedback unless harm to a patient occurs'],
 'The Code expects honest and constructive feedback to colleagues to help them improve their practice and performance.'),
('A nurse notices that two patients with similar names are on the same ward. What should be done?',
 'Alert the team; use name alerts and IDs', ['Rely on memory alone', 'Use bed numbers only', 'Label the beds with only the first name of each patient'],
 'A name alert and use of two identifiers prevent mix-ups and the team is made aware of the risk.'),
("What should a nurse do at the end of a shift when a patient's condition is changing?",
 'Give a clear handover and flag the concern', ['Leave a brief note without speaking', 'Wait for the next nurse to notice the change herself', 'Document only the vital signs taken'],
 'A direct handover ensures the oncoming nurse understands what to watch and what has been done, with the concern clearly communicated.'),
('What does effective teamwork in an emergency usually require?',
 'Closed-loop communication and clear roles', ['Everyone giving orders at the same time', 'Silence unless a doctor asks', 'Waiting for the manager before acting'],
 'Closed-loop communication repeats and confirms instructions, and a clear leader and roles reduce errors.'),
('An assistant tells a nurse that a patient refused his meal. What does the nurse do?',
 'Reassess the patient and find the cause', ['Record it and take no action', 'Tell the assistant to feed him anyway', 'Wait to see if it continues at the next meal'],
 'The nurse remains responsible for assessing, deciding on action and following up on information from the team.'),
('Which situation requires the nurse to involve a senior or manager?',
 'An unsafe staffing level', ['A routine change of shift', 'A request for a day off next week', "A patient's meal preference"],
 'Nurses raise concerns about unsafe staffing or conditions with the appropriate authority so patient safety is protected.'),
]
